    64	
    65	## 2. Task (1): the (B3) group, and which cusp theorems survive
    66	
    67	### 2.1 The presentation, derived — and the charge's claim corrected
    68	
    69	Scope first. MI's `(B3)` says `A_F` has **a cusp** — MI §6 defines "cusp" in
    70	the wide sense, *any unibranch singular germ*. The charge says
    71	*quasi-homogeneous* cusp. That is a strictly narrower hypothesis: in case (A)
    72	Lin-Zaidenberg supplies `{x^p=y^q}` and quasi-homogeneity is free, but in (B3)
    73	`A_F` is not homeomorphic to `C` and Lin-Zaidenberg does **not** apply. I
    74	work in the charge's scope and flag every use:
    75	
    76	```text
    77	SCOPE[B3-QH].  The cusp p_0 is assumed quasi-homogeneous, of type {x^p = y^q},
    78	gcd(p,q)=1, p,q >= 2.  NOT banked from MI; charged assumption.  For a general
    79	unibranch germ the local group is an iterated-torus-knot group and Sec 2.4
    80	generalises through its JSJ/Seifert pieces; that generalisation is NOT done here.
    81	```
    82	
    83	Now the presentation. Let `n = deg_y bar A_F` for a generic projection
    84	`pr:(x,y) |-> x`. Zariski-van Kampen gives `G = <x_1,...,x_n | R>` with `R`
    85	collected over the discriminant points:
    86	
    87	* **ordinary vertical tangency** (`v` of them): `x_i = x_j`;
    88	* **double point of two smooth branches with contact `t`** (`A_{2t-1}`; local
    89	  braid `sigma^{2t}`): `(x_i x_j)^t = (x_j x_i)^t`. For `t = 1` this **is** the
    90	  commuting square `[x_i,x_j] = 1`; for `t >= 2` it is not;
    91	* **the quasi-homogeneous cusp** (local braid `(sigma_1...sigma_{p-1})^q` on the
    92	  `p` strands through it): the `(p,q)` torus-braid relations.
    93	
    94	So the charge's sentence is right about *which* relators the double points
    95	contribute — commuting squares, exactly when the double points are genuine
    96	nodes — and wrong about the global shape:
    97	
    98	> **CORRECTION.** `G` is **not** `G_{p,q}` with `k` relators adjoined. `G` has
    99	> `n` generators, `n` being the degree of the projected curve, which is
   100	> unbounded in `p,q,k`; the cusp's relators involve only `p` of them; and the
   101	> tangency relations `x_i = x_j` are part of the data. What is canonical is
   102	> the **homomorphism** `iota : Loc_{p_0} = G_{p,q} -> G` induced by inclusion,
   103	> which is in general neither injective nor surjective. HT §2.2 (THEOREM
   104	> NO-PUSHFORWARD, CONFIRMED by HR §1) is exactly the statement that this
   105	> `iota` may destroy information, so nothing may be transported across it
   106	> by analogy.
   107	
   108	The consequence is methodological and it is the reason the rest of this section
   109	works: **every (B3) theorem below is either global-and-abelian (§2.2) or
   110	strictly local at a singular point (§2.3-§2.5), and none crosses `iota` in the
   111	forbidden direction.** Locally, `rho ∘ iota` is a genuine `S_N`-representation
   112	of `G_{p,q}` and everything HT proved about such representations applies.
   113	
   114	### 2.2 CUSP-PARITY survives, in a stronger form
   115	
   116	> **THEOREM B3-DEGREE.** Let `A_F` be irreducible (H2), `G = pi_1(C^2\A_F)`,
   117	> `rho : G -> S_N` the ACS-1 monodromy, `m` a meridian. By LEMMA F1
   118	> (HT §2.1, proved there) `G^{ab} = Z<m>`; write `deg : G -> Z` for the
   119	> abelianisation. Then for **every** `h ∈ G`
   120	> ```text
   121	>       sgn rho(h) = eps^{deg h} ,      eps = sgn rho(m) = (-1)^{W - sum_l s_l}.
   122	> ```
   123	> *Proof.* `sgn ∘ rho : G -> {+-1}` is a homomorphism to an abelian group, so it
   124	> factors through `G^{ab} = Z<m>` and is determined by its value on `m`; that
   125	> value is `(-1)^{N - #cycles}` for the cycle type `1^a prod_l mu_l^{s_l}`
   126	> of MI `[P3]` + 7.B, i.e. `(-1)^{W - sum_l s_l}`. ∎
   127	
   128	This is HT's CUSP-PARITY with the case-(A) hypothesis deleted. It contains
   129	HT's two identities as the special cases `h = alpha` (`deg = q`) and
   130	`h = beta` (`deg = p`) once `iota` is applied, and it applies to elements HT
   131	had no name for. Two uses below:
   132	
   133	* `deg z = pq` for the central `z = alpha^p` of a `(p,q)` cusp, so
   134	  `sgn rho(z) = eps^{pq}`;
   135	* at a double point with contact `t`, the two branch meridians have `deg = 1`
   136	  and their product has `deg = 2`, so `rho(g_1 g_2)` is always even.
   137	
   138	### 2.3 CENTRAL-RANK: refuted globally, survives locally
   139	
   140	HT's CENTRAL-RANK (`rank H^{ab} = r+1`, hence `r = j-1`) is a theorem about a
   141	**central** extension `1 -> Z_H -> H -> Delta -> 1`, and the centre is
   142	`Z(G_{p,q})`. In (B3) `G` is not `G_{p,q}` and has no such centre: `A_F` is not
   143	a cone, `C^2 \ A_F` does not retract to a Seifert-fibred `S^3`-complement, and
   144	there is no candidate `Z`. So:
   145	
   146	```text
   147	CENTRAL-RANK, global form:  REFUTED for (B3) (hypothesis absent, not merely
   148	                            unverified).  It must not be quoted at (B3).
   149	```
   150	
   151	What survives is the **per-orbit** statement inside the cusp's own local group,
   152	and it acquires a geometric meaning that case (A) did not display:
   153	
   154	> **LEMMA B3-LOC.** Let `p_0` be the cusp, `Loc_{p_0} ≅ G_{p,q}`, and let
   155	> `O` be an orbit of `rho(Loc_{p_0})` on the `N` sheets, `H_O` the point
   156	> stabiliser in `G_{p,q}`, `Delta_O := H_O Z/Z <= Z/p * Z/q` of index `M_O`
   157	> with Kurosh free rank `r_O`. Then `rank H_O^{ab} = r_O + 1`, and
   158	> ```text
   159	>   r_O + 1 = rank H_1(U_O),   U_O := the connected component of
   160	>             F^{-1}(B_{p_0} \ A_F) indexed by O.
   161	> ```
   162	> Moreover **if `O` is one of the `a_{p_0}` affine orbits** (`|O| = 1`, `F` a
   163	> local biholomorphism there), `U_O = B_y \ E` for the corresponding
   164	> `y ∈ F^{-1}(p_0)`, so
   165	> ```text
   166	>   r_O + 1 = # local branches of E at y  = # local branches of A_F at p_0 = 1,
   167	> ```
   168	> hence `r_O = 0` at every affine orbit over a unibranch point.
   169	>
   170	> *Proof.* The first sentence is HT CENTRAL-RANK applied to `H_O <= G_{p,q}`
   171	> (its proof uses only `H_2(Delta_O) = 0` and `Z_H ≅ Z`, both intact). For the
   172	> second: orbits of `rho(Loc_{p_0})` correspond to connected components of
   173	> `F^{-1}(B_{p_0}\A_F)`, and `H_O^{ab} = H_1(U_O)`. For the third: a Keller map
   174	> is étale, so `F` is a local biholomorphism at every `y`, `(E,y) ≅ (A_F,p_0)`
   175	> analytically, and `H_1(B_y \ E) = Z^{#branches}` (link of a plane germ with
   176	> `r` branches is an `r`-component link). ∎
   177	
   178	The last clause is the (B3) replacement for `j`: in case (A) `r = j-1` reads
   179	off the **global** component count; in (B3) the same algebra reads off a
   180	**local branch count**, which is pinned by MI's profile data.
   181	
   182	### 2.4 THEOREM B3-CAGE — ORBIFOLD-CAGE, orbit-summed
   183	
   184	> **THEOREM B3-CAGE.** At a quasi-homogeneous cusp `p_0` of type `(p,q)`, put
   185	> `A := rho(alpha)`, `B := rho(beta)` (images of the two `G_{p,q}` generators
   186	> under `rho ∘ iota`), `c_A := #cycles(A)`, `c_B := #cycles(B)` on the `N`
   187	> sheets. Let the orbits of `<A,B>` be `O_1,...,O_T`, `kappa_i` the order of
   188	> `rho(z)|_{O_i}` and `M_i = |O_i|/kappa_i`, `M_tot := sum_i M_i`. Then
   189	> ```text
   190	>   (B3-1)   c_A + c_B  =  M_tot + T - R_tot ,     R_tot := sum_i r_{O_i} ;
   191	>   (B3-2)   T = # F^{-1}(p_0) ∪ {infinity pieces} = # parts of the fibre
   192	>            partition over p_0, with part sizes |O_i| ;
   193	>   (B3-3)   r_{O_i} = 0 for each of the a_{p_0} affine orbits (LEMMA B3-LOC),
   194	>            so  c_A + c_B <= M_tot + T,  with the defect carried entirely by
   195	>            the pieces at infinity ;
   196	>   (B3-4)   the nontrivial cone orders {p/m > 1} ∪ {q/l > 1} attached to one
   197	>            orbit are pairwise coprime, and coprime to that orbit's kappa_i.
   198	> ```
   199	> *Proof.* `rho(z)` is central in `<A,B>`, hence semiregular on each orbit, so
   200	> `O_i` splits into `M_i` blocks and `Z/p * Z/q` acts on the block set `𝔅`,
   201	> `|𝔅| = M_tot`, giving a degree-`M_tot` (possibly disconnected) orbifold cover
   202	> of `D^2(p,q)`. Multiplicativity of `chi^{orb}`,
   203	> `sum_c (2-2g_c-c_c) - S - S' = -M_tot` with `S,S'` the numbers of preimages of
   204	> the two cone points, plus `S = c_A`, `S' = c_B` (HT (C-4), whose proof is
   205	> per-orbit and needs no transitivity), plus `2g_c + c_c = r_{O_c}+1`, give
   206	> (B3-1). (B3-2) is the orbit/component correspondence; (B3-3) is LEMMA B3-LOC;
   207	> (B3-4) is HT (C-2)-(C-3) applied orbitwise. ∎
   208	
   209	At `T = 1` this is HT's `s+s' = M+2-j` verbatim. The generalisation is exactly
   210	what (B3) needs, because the cusp's local monodromy in (B3) is never
   211	transitive: some sheets survive affinely and some are lost at infinity.
   212	
   213	### 2.5 THEOREM B3-PUSHOFF and THEOREM B3-COMPONENT
   214	
   215	`(B3)` needs one object case (A) never had: the covering of the curve itself.
   216	Let `A_F°` be the smooth locus of `A_F` pulled back to the normalisation
   217	`A_F~ ≅ A^1` (MI Lemma A), i.e. `A^1` minus one puncture per branch at each
   218	singular point. `F` étale makes
   219	
   220	```text
   221	(2.5.1)   E° := F^{-1}(A_F°)  -->  A_F°     an a-sheeted covering,
   222	```
   223	
   224	`a = a_p` at every smooth `p` (MI control 2). Its monodromy is not `rho`, but
   225	it is computed by `rho`:
   226	
   227	> **THEOREM B3-PUSHOFF.** For a loop `gamma` in `A_F°` let `gamma~` be its
   228	> push-off into `C^2 \ A_F`. Then `rho(gamma~)` centralises `rho(m_gamma)` and
   229	> acts on `Fix rho(m_gamma)` — the `a` sheets — as the monodromy of (2.5.1).
   230	> At the puncture attached to a branch `b` at a singular point `P`,
   231	> ```text
   232	>   gamma~  =  z_b · prod_{b' != b} g_{b'}^{(b · b')_P}     in  pi_1(B_P \ A_F),
   233	> ```
   234	> `z_b = 1` for a **smooth** branch `b`. In particular:
   235	> * at a double point of two smooth branches with contact `t`,
   236	>   `gamma~ = g_{other}^t`;
   237	> * at a unibranch point, `rho(gamma~)` lies in the centre of
   238	>   `rho(Loc_P)`.
   239	>
   240	> *Proof.* The first sentence is the standard identification of the fibre of
   241	> (2.5.1) over `p` with `Fix rho(m_p)`. For a smooth branch `b`, `gamma` bounds
   242	> a disc **inside** `b`; pushing that disc off normally gives a disc meeting
   243	> `A_F` exactly in the other branches, with multiplicity `(b·b')_P` each, and
   244	> `lk(gamma~, b) = 0`; so `gamma~` is the stated product. For a unibranch
   245	> singular branch, `rho(gamma~)` commutes with the images of all local
   246	> meridians, which generate `rho(Loc_P)`. ∎
   247	
   248	> **THEOREM B3-COMPONENT.** `j := #`components of `E` equals the number of
   249	> orbits of the groupoid generated by (i) the monodromy of (2.5.1) on the `a`
   250	> sheets and (ii) for each `y ∈ E` lying over a singular point `P`, the
   251	> identification of the `r_P` branch-sheets at `y`.
   252	>
   253	> *Proof.* `E = E° ∪ (E ∩ F^{-1}(Sing A_F))`. Removing a point from an
   254	> irreducible curve keeps it connected, so components of `E` and of `E°` agree
   255	> except for the merges induced by the points `y`, at which `E` has `r_P`
   256	> branches (`F` étale), one per branch of `A_F` at `P`. ∎
   257	
   258	`j` is exactly `rank H^{ab}` (LEMMA F1), i.e. the quantity that carried the
   259	whole case-(A) argument. B3-COMPONENT computes it without any centre.
   260	
   261	## 3. Task (2): the cage on the (B3) profile
   262	
   263	### 3.1 `N = 4`: the representation is pinned
   264	
   265	N4-PIN (MI §8.1) gives `a=2`, `W=2`, one dicritical `(1,2)`, generic meridian
   266	cycle type `(2,1,1)`, fibre partition `(3,1)` at the cusp `p_0` with
   267	`a_{p_0}=1`, and `(2,2)` at each double point with `a_q = 0`. Then:
   268	
   269	> **PROPOSITION 3.1.** `rho(G) = S_4`; `rho(Loc_{p_0}) ≅ S_3` acting on the
   270	> 3-element orbit and fixing the sheet `y_0` over the cusp; at each double
   271	> point `rho(g_1), rho(g_2)` are **disjoint** transpositions.
   272	>
   273	> *Proof.* `A_F` irreducible makes all meridians conjugate, so `rho(G)` is
   274	> generated by the transpositions conjugate to `rho(m)`; a transitive subgroup
   275	> of `S_4` generated by transpositions is `S_4`. At `p_0` the orbits are
   276	> `(3,1)`, so every element of `rho(Loc_{p_0})` preserves both, so the
   277	> generating transpositions lie inside the 3-set; transitive there, hence
   278	> `S_3`. At a double point the two generators are commuting transpositions;
   279	> equal ones give orbits `(2,1,1)`, disjoint ones give `(2,2)`. For contact
   280	> `t >= 2` the alternative "sharing a letter" forces `3|t` and orbits `(3,1)`;
   281	> `(2,2)` excludes it. ∎
   282	
   283	`|rho(G)| = 24 > 4`, so MI COR 7.2 (non-regularity) is **satisfied, not
   284	violated**: it kills nothing at `N = 4` in (B3).
   285	
   286	> **PROPOSITION 3.2 (the cusp type at `N = 4`).** The Puiseux pair of the (B3)
   287	> cusp satisfies
   288	> ```text
   289	>        (2 | p  and  3 | q)      or      (3 | p  and  2 | q).
   290	> ```
   291	> *Proof.* `rho(z)` is central in `rho(Loc_{p_0}) = S_3`, which has trivial
   292	> centre, so `rho(z) = e`; hence `ord(A) | p`, `ord(B) | q`, coprime, with
   293	> `<A,B> = S_3`. Two elements of coprime orders generating `S_3` must have
   294	> orders `2` and `3` (neither can be trivial, `S_3` being non-cyclic). ∎
   295	>
   296	> *Independent parity check.* `deg z = pq` and `rho(z)=e` is even, so
   297	> THEOREM B3-DEGREE gives `eps^{pq} = +1` with `eps = (-1)^{W - sum s_l} =
   298	> (-1)^{2-1} = -1`, i.e. **`pq` even** — the weaker half of Prop 3.2, obtained
   299	> from the parity law alone. The two routes agree.
   300	
   301	This kills, for instance, `(p,q) = (2,5), (3,5), (4,5), (5,6), (5,7)` and
   302	every pair with `3 ∤ pq`; it admits `(2,3)`, `(3,4)`, `(2,9)`, `(4,3)`,
   303	`(8,3)`, `(2,15)`, `(4,9)`, ... It is a **necessary numerical gate**, in the
   304	Path-1 discipline sense: no realisation of any admitted pair is claimed.
   305	
   306	**B3-CAGE, checked on the admitted cell.** With `A` a transposition and `B` a
   307	3-cycle in `S_{\{1,2,3\}}`: `c_A = 3`, `c_B = 2`, `kappa_i = 1`, `M_tot = 4`,
   308	`T = 2`. (B3-1) gives `R_tot = 4 + 2 - 5 = 1`, and (B3-3) gives `r = 0` at the
   309	affine orbit `{y_0}`, so the size-3 orbit at infinity has `r = 1`, i.e.
   310	`H_1(U) = Z^2`. Independent orbifold count: the degree-3 cover of `D^2(p,q)`
   311	with `A`-cycles `(2,1)` and `B`-cycle `(3)` has
   312	`chi^{orb} = chi(Sigma) - 3 + 3/p + 3/q = 3(1/p+1/q-1)`, so `chi(Sigma) = 0`,
   313	`Sigma` = annulus, `r = 2g+c-1 = 1`. **Agrees.**
   314	
   315	### 3.2 `N = 4`: `E` is determined completely
   316	
   317	Apply B3-PUSHOFF at each puncture of `A_F~ = A^1`. The punctures are `P_0`
