     1	# AS109 exact-lift partial-`y` floor twelve — theorem-interface composition
     2	
     3	Date: 2026-08-26
     4	
     5	Producer: Opus 5 blind ideation lane, extracted into
     6	`xmodel/ideation-20260826T2230Z-opus5-blind-extract.md`.
     7	
     8	## Claim
     9	
    10	Let
    11	
    12	```text
    13	P = x - x^109 + 109*A,
    14	Q = y + 109*B
    15	```
    16	
    17	belong to `Z_109[x,y]`, and suppose `det J(P,Q)=1`.  Then
    18	
    19	```text
    20	max(deg_y(A),deg_y(B)) >= 12.
    21	```
    22	
    23	## Proof
    24	
    25	If both correction degrees were at most eleven, then over `Q_109`
    26	
    27	```text
    28	deg_y(P) <= 11,
    29	deg_y(Q) <= max(1,deg_y(B)) <= 11.
    30	```
    31	
    32	The promoted maximum-eleven theorem therefore makes `(P,Q)` a polynomial
    33	automorphism over `Q_109`.  But the reviewed AS109 Hensel bridge says that
    34	each of the 109 source balls `(a,b)+109*Z_109^2` maps bijectively to the same
    35	target ball `(0,b)+109*Z_109^2`; hence the map is noninjective over `Q_109`.
    36	Contradiction.
    37	
    38	## Scope
    39	
    40	This is conditional on an exact integral polynomial lift in the displayed
    41	AS109 coordinates.  Partial `y`-degree is not invariant under arbitrary
    42	source changes.  The conclusion supplies no lift, support bound, marked
    43	collision, routing into an exact degree-twelve cell, arbitrary-support no-go,
    44	counterexample, or JC2 result.
