   384	### 3.4 Lemma CONTACT: components of `A_F` are forced into high mutual contact
   385	
   386	> **Lemma CONTACT `[D]`.** Let `D_1, D_2` be distinct components with Chau data
   387	> `(d,e)` and multiplicities `m_1, m_2`. Then
   388	> ```text
   389	>   sum_{p in C^2} (D_1.D_2)_p  <=  m_1 m_2 d e - min(m_1, m_2) ,
   390	>   (D_1.D_2)_{P_inf}           >=  m_1 m_2 max(d,e) |d - e| + min(m_1, m_2) .
   391	> ```
   392	
   393	*Proof.* `sum_{p affine} (D_1.D_2)_p = deg_xi f_1(a_2(xi), b_2(xi))`, since the
   394	`D_2`-parametrisation is proper and birational. Grade `C[u,v]` by
   395	`wt(u) = d, wt(v) = e`; `f_1` has weighted degree `m_1 de` with weighted-leading
   396	form `(B^d u^e - A^e v^d)^{m_1}` (SS3.3). The substitution sends a monomial of
   397	weighted degree `w` to a polynomial of `xi`-degree at most `m_2 w`, since
   398	`deg a_2 = m_2 d` and `deg b_2 = m_2 e`. The top level contributes at most
   399	`m_1 (m_2 de - 1)` because `B^d a_2^e - A^e b_2^d` loses its leading coefficient
   400	exactly by C7; every lower level contributes at most `m_2(m_1 de - 1)`. Take the
   401	max of the two, i.e. subtract `min(m_1,m_2)`. Bezout in `P^2` with
   402	`deg D_i = m_i max(d,e)` and `max(d,e)^2 - de = max(d,e)|d-e|` gives the second. []
   403	
   404	*Controls `[C]`.* `(9,6,2)` against `m_2 = 1` companions: bound `17`, actual `12`;
   405	contact bound `>= 10`, actual `15`; Bezout `27 = 12 + 15`. Against two independent
   406	`m_2 = 2` companions: bound `34`, actual `33` both times; contact bound `>= 20`,
   407	actual `21` both times. Near-sharp, never violated.
