    93	## 2. Foundations, custody-first
    94	
    95	### 2.1 (F1) Gysin freeness — proved, not cited
    96	
    97	> **LEMMA F1.** Let `E ⊂ C^2` be a curve with `r` irreducible components.
    98	> Then `H_1(C^2 \ E; Z) ≅ Z^r`, free, with the meridians as a basis.
    99	>
   100	> *Proof.* Let `f_1,...,f_r` be reduced defining polynomials of the
   101	> components. `H_1(C^2\E;Z)` is generated by meridians `mu_1,...,mu_r`
   102	> (Zariski–van Kampen: the group is generated by meridians of the
   103	> components, and conjugate meridians agree in `H_1`). The winding-number
   104	> homomorphism `w = (deg f_1,...,deg f_r) : H_1(C^2\E) -> Z^r`, induced
   105	> by `(f_1,...,f_r) : C^2\E -> (C^*)^r`, sends `mu_i` to the `i`-th basis
   106	> vector. A generating set mapping to a basis under a homomorphism to a
   107	> free module is itself a basis. ∎
   108	
   109	This is the exact statement ID §3.2 uses and the exact statement MPRIME
   110	§7 consumes inside CUSP-CAGE (`H^ab = Z^j` torsion-free). It needs no
   111	Libgober, no characteristic variety, and no `P^2` bookkeeping. Recorded
   112	here as proved so that neither lane rides an uncited step.
   113	
   114	### 2.2 (F2) THEOREM NO-PUSHFORWARD — the charged direction is wrong
   115	
   116	Two candidate local-to-global maps appear in the charge and in DISC.
   117	Call them:
   118	
   119	* `(M-surj)` the **link-at-infinity** map `Gamma_infty := pi_1(S^3_R \ K_infty) -> G := pi_1(C^2 \ A)`;
   120	* `(M-loc)`  the **local germ** map `Loc_P := pi_1(B_P \ (Dbar ∪ L_infty)) -> G`, `P ∈ Dbar ∩ L_infty`.
   121	
   122	> **THEOREM NO-PUSHFORWARD.** Let `phi : Gamma -> G` be a homomorphism,
   123	> `rho : G -> S_N` transitive, `H := rho^{-1}(Stab_1)`,
   124	> `Lambda := (rho ∘ phi)^{-1}(Stab_1)`.
   125	>
   126	> (i) If `phi` is **surjective**, then `phi(Lambda) = H` and
   127	> `Lambda^{ab} ↠ H^{ab}`. Torsion in `Lambda^{ab}` therefore *constrains*
   128	> `H^{ab}` only by being killable: a quotient of a group with torsion
   129	> need have none.
   130	>
   131	> (ii) If `phi` is **not surjective**, `phi(Lambda) <= H` and there is a
   132	> map `Lambda^{ab} -> H^{ab}` with no injectivity in sight; torsion in
   133	> the source is invisible in the target.
   134	>
   135	> In neither case does "torsion locally" imply "torsion globally". ∎
   136	
   137	This is not a technicality to be routed around. The global group is
   138	obtained from the local one by *imposing more relations*, and imposing
   139	relations can only shrink `H^{ab}`; torsion is exactly what shrinking
   140	destroys.
   141	
   142	**Witness, MEASURED, inside the campaign's own local model.** Take
   143	`Gamma = B_3` — the trefoil group, i.e. the `(2,3)` cabled place, the
   144	model whose cable DISC identifies as the torsion source. Take the
   145	transitive `rho : B_3 -> S_4`, `sigma_1 |-> (1234)`, `sigma_2 |-> (1243)`
   146	(0-based `(1,2,3,0)`, `(1,3,0,2)`), for which `H_1(cover) = Z (+) Z/2`.
   147	Adjoin the single relator `w = (sigma_1 sigma_2^{-1})^3`, which `rho`
   148	already kills. Then
   149	
   150	```text
   151	H_1 of the cover of  B_3                            =  Z (+) Z/2
   152	H_1 of the cover of  B_3 / <<(s1 s2^-1)^3>>         =  Z          MEASURED
   153	```
   154	
   155	Four length-6 relators do this (the shortest; the other three are its
