+2. On the residue line, factor the `SL_2(C[Z])` matrix of Section 4 and expose
+   its common partial quotient `H`. Substitute before expansion. This separates
+   genuine terminal data from the arbitrary Bezout/continued-fraction freedom.
+3. At a generic top `A`-level, form the coefficient matrix on only the newest
+   coefficient block, over `Q(e,U,j)` with order index `j`. Compute its maximal
+   minors fraction-free and branch on every vanished pivot. The existing formal
+   theorem says bottom-up levels are always solvable with one free `C[Z]`
+   parameter; therefore a proof must come from the terminal boundary, not from
+   another low-order obstruction
