+## 11. Allocation, falsifiers, and close
+
+Allocate frontier effort in this order: (1) full-source O0/O1 plus the generic
+block/`SL_2` discriminator; (2) the explicit case-(A) `N=8` global subgroup
+gate; (3) a preflighted profile-first `N=6` census, with the cheap prime-seven
+scout only if it reduces the curve queue; (4) one `Gamma` B3 regression; (5)
+joint companion/source-open completion only after a full cover survives.
+
+The portfolio changes direction on clear falsifiers. If the common quotient
+`H` is visible to the full equations but yields no uniform pivot, stop calling
+`U` gauge and run the construction recurrence. If a global N8 subgroup passes
+every marked/twisted test, switch that lane from exclusion to source-open
+construction. If the preflight cannot certify the curve encoding, launch no
+solver. If a degree-four regression merely fails at an already-known binding
+route, archive it rather than re-proving `N=4`.
+
+The central conclusion is deliberately negative about architecture: exact
