+
+## 4. New mechanism and cross-connection
+
+### `A2-SL2-DESCENT` — a genuinely new mechanism
+
+On a live ray cell, `[RK]` declares
+
+```text
+n = U-e,       sigma = (U+e)/2,
+deg q = U,     deg s = sigma,
+deg r' = n-1,  deg p' = 3n/2-1,
+2(q r' - p' s) = kappa != 0.
+```
+
+Here `p'` and `r'` are source **variable labels**, not derivatives; below every
+derivative is written `partial_Z`.
+
+Thus
+
+```text
+             [ q   p' ]
+M(Z) =       [ s   r' ]       has det M = kappa/2 in C*.
+```
+
+This is not an analogy: after a constant rescaling, `M` is an element of
+`SL_2(C[Z])`. On every surviving cell `U>=3e+2`, the two degree differences are
+the same nonnegative integer
+
+```text
+t := deg(p')-deg(q) = deg(r')-deg(s) = (U-3e-2)/2.
+```
+
+Moreover
+
+```text
+r'/s - p'/q = kappa/(2qs).
+```
+
+The right side is strictly proper at infinity, so the polynomial parts of
+`p'/q` and `r'/s` coincide. Call that common quotient `H(Z)`, `deg H=t`, and
+write
+
+```text
+p' = Hq + p_0,       r' = Hs + r_0.
+```
+
+Then `q r_0-p_0 s=kappa/2`; equivalently the elementary column operation
+`C_2 <- C_2-H C_1` preserves the unit determinant and strictly reduces the
+second column. Repeating the Euclidean algorithm gives a continued-
+fraction/elementary-matrix word for `M`. `E0` alone permits arbitrary word
+length; any Mason–Stothers bound must therefore consume other equations.
+
+Before choosing a complement, O0/O1 give a sharper compatibility test. Put
+
+```text
+W = q partial_Z(s) - partial_Z(q) s,
+T = -(C1 partial_Z(E1)-partial_Z(C1) E1)/2
+    -3 D1 partial_Z(s)/4 + partial_Z(D1) s/4 - ZW/2.
+```
+
+Then E0, O0, and O1 are exactly the following three affine rows in the two
+source variables `(r',p')`:
+
+```text
+[q,  -s ] [r',p']^t = kappa/2,
+[C1,-E1] [r',p']^t = -W/2,
+[C2,-E2] [r',p']^t = T.
+```
+
+Their augmented determinant must vanish, giving the source-exact identity
+
+```text
+D := (s C1-q E1)T + (W/2)(s C2-q E2)
+     + (kappa/2)(E1 C2-C1 E2) = 0.                 (DET-EO)
+```
+
+This uses only the reviewed equations
+`[xmodel/cell-32-spec-sol56-20260901.md:82-107]` and already yields a theorem.
+Set `A0=sC1-qE1`, `A1=sC2-qE2`, `Tbar=T+ZW/2`, and `B=A1-ZA0`. Then
+
+```text
+(W/2)B + A0 Tbar + (kappa/2)(E1 C2-C1 E2) = 0.    (BD)
+```
+
+On RAY-2, `deg W=U+sigma-1` with nonzero leader
+`q_U s_sigma(sigma-U)`, while `deg A0=U+2e`,
+`deg Tbar<=sigma+3e-1`, and
+`deg(E1 C2-C1 E2)<=sigma+3e+1`. Therefore **`deg B<=5e` uniformly in
+`U`**; if the right side of `(BD)` vanishes, the stronger conclusion is
+`B=0`. Using the exact wall formulas `[RK:146-160]`, this bounded defect is
+
+```text
+4b^2 eta (B/Z)
+ = (E1-b eta^2)(4b^2 eta q-3a s^2-2c5 eta s)
+   + 2c5 eta s E1,                   deg(left side) <= 6e-1.
+```
+
+Thus the full O0/O1 equations turn unbounded-degree `q` into a polynomial
+approximation with defect bounded solely by `e`. Since E0 also gives
+`gcd(q,s)=1`, resultant/gcd consequences of `(BD)` are now the cheapest route
+to a real `U` bound.
+
+For computation, take coefficient normal forms of `(BD)` in the declared ray
+quotient localized only at its charged opens; branch on every vanished leader
+and on zero. Use `(1,5)`, `(1,7)`, and `(2,8)` as controls. If it passes, choose
+Euclidean remainders `deg p_0<deg q`, `deg r_0<deg s` and test
+`A0 | 4(C1 r_0-p_0 E1)+2W` to reconstruct `H`. A nonzero constant kills only
+its declared branch; another polynomial is a constraint, not emptiness; exact
+identities are only a formal-family signal.
+
+This cross-connects Avenue 29's Hamiltonian identities with Avenue 5's
+Jung/elementary descent at an exact one-variable PID client. It also enforces
+the raw-degree guardrail: an elementary factorization of `M` is not yet a legal
+source or target automorphism of `F`. The lane must exhibit the map from the
+matrix column operation to the original normal-form variables before declaring
+`U` gauge.
